course: biology
lesson: 120
title: How real mice survive: squeezing, climbing and hiding
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Almost everyone wants to eat a mouse: cats, owls, foxes, weasels, snakes and hawks. So a mouse's whole life is about not being seen, not being caught and having lots of babies.
explain: A real mouse runs along the edge of the kitchen wall, not across the open floor, with its whiskers brushing the wall, so a crack or a pipe to escape into is always close.
explain: At the first strange noise it freezes, because a still mouse is hard to spot; if danger comes closer it bolts, jumps, climbs up a rough wall or vanishes up the inside of a pipe.
explain: Unlike Jerry, a real mouse cannot outsmart a cat with an iron, but it has its own tricks: teeth that never stop growing, songs too high for us to hear, and secret food stores for the winter.
fact: Mice keep their whiskers touching a wall as they run and avoid open floors, and pest experts put traps along walls because mice travel there.
fact: A house mouse can jump about 30 centimetres straight up, many times its own height, and can climb rough walls and run along thin wires using its tail for balance.
fact: Mice sweep their whiskers back and forth many times a second to feel the shape of things around them, which matters because they are mostly active at night.
fact: Many small animals freeze first and run second, because many hunters notice movement more than shape; mice even avoid the smell of cats they have never met.
fact: Mice have four front teeth, the incisors, which keep growing all their lives and are worn down by gnawing; mice can gnaw wood, plastic and even electric cables, which can cause fires; rats, squirrels, beavers and rabbits also have front teeth that never stop growing.
fact: A mother mouse can have a litter of about six to eight babies every few weeks; the babies are born pink and blind and can look after themselves at about three weeks old.
fact: Mice call to each other in sounds too high for people to hear, called ultrasound, and male mice sing tiny songs that scientists record and slow down.
fact: Owls, foxes, cats, weasels, snakes and hawks all hunt mice, and a family of barn owls can eat over a thousand mice and voles in a year.
fact: Wood mice and field mice store seeds, nuts and berries in burrows or under logs for winter, and forgotten seeds can grow into new plants.
fact: Mice groom themselves many times a day, licking their paws and wiping their faces, ears and whiskers, but wild mice can still carry germs that make people ill.
fact: Mice use pipes, cables and gaps behind kitchen units as hidden roads, and may visit the same food many times in a night, taking only tiny bits each time.
exercise: Why does a mouse run along the wall instead of across the floor? | A wall protects one side and leads to cracks for a quick escape | Mice cannot run on smooth floors, so their feet slip unless they touch a wall | Mice like the wallpaper | Mice are scared of the colour of floors and only trust walls
exercise: Where do pest experts put mouse traps? | Along the walls | In the middle of the room | Upside down on the ceiling | On the dinner table
exercise: About how high can a house mouse jump straight up? | About 30 centimetres | About 3 centimetres | About 3 metres | Mice cannot jump at all
exercise: What does a mouse use its tail for when running along a thin wire? | Balance | Grabbing food | Swimming in water | Talking to friends
exercise: Why do mice sweep their whiskers back and forth? | To feel the shape of things around them in the dark | To keep flies and little midges away from their eyes and nose | To dry their face | To look tough to cats
exercise: What does a mouse usually do first when it hears a strange noise? | Freezes still | Squeaks as loud as it can | Runs straight at the noise | Falls fast asleep
exercise: Why does freezing help a mouse hide? | Many hunters notice movement more than shape | Mice turn invisible when still | Hunters cannot smell a still mouse | Freezing makes the mouse colder, so it shrinks and becomes smaller
exercise: Why do mice gnaw hard things like cardboard? | Their front teeth never stop growing and gnawing wears them down | They are always angry and bite things to calm themselves down | They are trying to sharpen their claws, not their teeth, on the hard edges | Cardboard is their favourite food
exercise: Why can mice gnawing in a house be dangerous? | They can chew electric cables, which can cause fires | They eat all the bricks | They make the house fall down overnight by eating the walls | They bite every single person they meet in the house, even in bed
exercise: How many babies are in a typical house mouse litter? | About six to eight | Always just one baby | About fifty at once | About two hundred babies
exercise: How old are baby mice when they can look after themselves? | About three weeks | Only about three days | About three whole years | About three months
exercise: What is ultrasound? | Sounds too high for people to hear | Very loud low sounds | Sounds made only by machines | Sounds that travel through stone only
exercise: Which animal sings tiny songs too high for us to hear? | The male mouse | The pet goldfish | The old tortoise | The garden earthworm
exercise: About how many mice and voles can a family of barn owls eat in a year? | Over a thousand | About ten | About one hundred thousand | None, owls eat fruit
exercise: Why does a field mouse store seeds in a hidden pantry? | Food is hard to find in winter | To grow a garden on purpose | To give them all to its friends | To sell them later
exercise: How do mice keep clean? | They groom many times a day, wiping their faces with their paws | They never clean at all | They bathe in puddles every hour, splashing about like little ducks | Other animals clean them
exercise: What are pipes and cables to a house mouse? | Hidden roads around the house | Places to sleep all winter | Food to eat | Nothing, mice avoid them
test: «saw one mouse in the shed last month, now there's loads, how??» What is the best answer? | Mice can have six to eight babies every few weeks | Mice split in two like cells | They were all hiding there quietly for years | The first mouse called all its friends from town to come and live there
test: «mice are filthy, they never wash» What is right? | Mice groom many times a day, though wild ones can still carry germs | Yes, mice never clean themselves | Mice wash only in the bath, when people leave some water in it | Mice are very clean, so even wild ones never carry any germs at all
test: Why should you wash your hands after cleaning a pet mouse's cage? | Mice and their droppings can carry germs | Mouse fur makes your hands too soft | The cage is made of poison | It makes the mouse happy
test: Lab mice that have never seen a cat still avoid a place that smells of one. Why would this help a wild mouse? | It can avoid danger before ever meeting a cat | It helps the mouse find the cheese | It makes cats like it | It does not help at all, because the smell means nothing to it
test: What would happen to barn owls if all the mice in an area disappeared? | They would struggle to find enough food | They would start eating grass | Nothing, owls never eat mice | They would grow bigger
test: «Jerry hides in a neat hole with a door, real mice too?» Where does a real mouse escape to? | Cracks, pipes and gaps behind kitchen units | A little arched door in the skirting board | A tiny bed with a quilt | The middle of the kitchen floor
test: «Stuart Little drives a little car and wears clothes» What is a real mouse's way of getting around fast? | Running along walls, climbing and jumping | Driving tiny cars along the skirting board | Riding on cats | Flying with its ears
test: How is a wood mouse like a gardener without knowing it? | Seeds it forgets in its stores can grow into new plants | It waters the plants with its long tail every night | It digs neat flower beds on purpose and plants bulbs in rows | It cuts the grass with its teeth
test: Why do scientists slow down their recordings of mouse songs? | The songs are too high for people to hear at normal speed | The mice sing far too fast for any scientist to write down | To make the mice sing better | Slow songs keep the mice warm
test: What other animals have front teeth that never stop growing, like mice? | Rats, squirrels, beavers and rabbits | Cats, dogs and lions | Snakes and lizards | Owls and hawks
test: «mouse fell in the tall bucket, it's stuck lol» Is it? | Maybe not, a mouse can jump about 30 centimetres straight up | Yes, mice can never jump at all, not even a few centimetres | Yes, mice are too heavy to climb, so it will stay there | No, mice can fly out
test: «5 mice in the cage, how many tails and how many legs, my sis says 5 and 10» | 5 tails and 20 legs | 5 tails and 10 legs | 10 tails and 20 legs | 0 tails and 20 legs
exam: Identify the principal function of the continuous gnawing behaviour exhibited by mice. | It wears down incisors which grow throughout life | It serves to communicate with other rodents | It is a manifestation of illness | It sharpens the claws of the forelimbs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reproductive capacity of the house mouse? | A female may produce litters of approximately six to eight young every few weeks | A female produces a single offspring once in her lifetime, after a long pregnancy of a year | The house mouse reproduces solely by division | Young mice are independent from the moment of birth
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vocal communication of mice. | Mice communicate in part by ultrasound, inaudible to the human ear | Mice are entirely silent creatures | Mice communicate solely by loud low-frequency calls that humans hear easily | Mice imitate the vocalisations of cats
exam: Determine the reason for which pest controllers position traps along walls rather than in open areas. | Mice habitually travel along walls and avoid open spaces | Walls emit a scent attractive to rodents | Traps are prohibited in the centre of rooms | Mice are incapable of crossing open floors
